"date":"2026-10-12","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-02","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-09","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-26","committee":"Governance Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-07","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-10","committee":"Governance Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-29","committee":"Governance Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"New","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-07","committee":"Notice of Exempt Cabinet Meetings","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Notice of Exempt Cabinet Meeting","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.dover.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Dover Council recovers just 4.7% of housing maintenance charges, despite audit warning","lead":"Dover District Council has recovered only \u00a39,189 from \u00a3194,731 in housing maintenance charges that it raised over four years, a recovery rate of 4.7 per cent. An audit review of the scheme, which raises charges when the council carries out repair works at tenants' homes, identified recovery failures and in March 2025 made a critical priority recommendation that the council pursue outstanding orders. That recommendation remains unimplemented. According to the council's quarterly internal audit update presented to its governance committee on 24 September, the recovery process \"remains outstanding\", with implementation now rescheduled to March 2027. Recovery has effectively stopped: in 2025-26, the council raised \u00a38,328 in charges but recovered nothing.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"DDC - Quarterly Internal Audit Update Report 24-09-2026","paper_url":"https://dover.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s68358/68358.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance Committee","paper_title":"DDC - Quarterly Internal Audit Update Report 24-09-2026","paper_url":"https://dover.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s68358/68358.pdf","headline":"PartnershipOne Business Rates Reliefs and Credits audit improved from Reasonable to Substantial assurance with only one medium-priority recommendation outstanding","detail":"Follow-up review shows improvement from Reasonable assurance (2 medium, 4 low recommendations) to Substantial assurance. Five of six original recommendations have been closed; only 1 low-priority recommendation remains outstanding. This represents significant progress in strengthening controls over business rates administration.","category":"audit","matters":"Improved controls over business rates administration reduce revenue leakage risk and strengthen financial integrity","figures":"Moved from Reasonable to Substantial assurance; 6 original recommendations reduced to 1 low-priority outstanding","quote":"PartnershipOne Limited \u2013 Business Rates Reliefs & Credits | Reasonable | Substantial","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance Committee","paper_title":"DDC - Quarterly Internal Audit Update Report 24-09-2026","paper_url":"https://dover.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s68358/68358.pdf","headline":"Counter Fraud Resilience Arrangements audit improved from Limited to Reasonable assurance, with four high-priority recommendations on track for 30 September 2026 completion","detail":"Follow-up review of Counter Fraud Resilience shows improvement from Limited assurance (3 critical, 6 high, 5 low recommendations) to Reasonable assurance with four high-priority recommendations remaining outstanding but on target. The four outstanding recommendations have not been escalated to committee because implementation is tracking to the 30 September 2026 deadline.","category":"audit","matters":"Strengthening fraud control systems reduces the council's exposure to financial loss from fraud and misappropriation","figures":"Moved from Limited to Reasonable assurance; 4 high-priority recommendations outstanding but on track","quote":"** Please Note the 4 Recommendations regarding above - e) 'Counter Fraud Resilience Arrangements' have not been escalated as they are on target and due to be implemented by 30 September 2026.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance Committee","paper_title":"DDC - Quarterly Internal Audit Update Report 24-09-2026","paper_url":"https://dover.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s68358/68358.pdf","headline":"Five council services retain Limited or No assurance ratings pending follow-up reviews, including Grounds Maintenance (No assurance) and Housing Repairs and Maintenance (Limited)","detail":"Annex 2 identifies five services not yet followed up following initial limited or no assurance audits: CCTV, Licensing, Grounds Maintenance, Housing Repairs and Maintenance, and Performance Management. These services contain documented control gaps that remain unresolved, with follow-up work listed as work-in-progress. Grounds Maintenance audit received No assurance, the lowest rating.","category":"audit","matters":"Unresolved control weaknesses across multiple housing and operational services could allow losses, errors or compliance failures to go undetected","figures":"5 services awaiting follow-up: CCTV (Substantial/Limited); Licensing (Reasonable/Limited); Grounds Maintenance (No); Housing Repairs and Maintenance (Limited); Performance Management (Limited)","quote":"SERVICES GIVEN LIMITED / NO ASSURANCE LEVELS YET TO BE REVIEWED","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance Committee","paper_title":"DDC - Quarterly Internal Audit Update Report 24-09-2026","paper_url":"https://dover.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s68358/68358.pdf","headline":"Dover District Council is owed \u00a3722,409.77 for administering the last three elections, with central government and county council payments taking up to two years to arrive","detail":"Electoral Registration and Election Management audit (Substantial assurance) identified that while the council's electoral processes are well-managed, payments from Kent County Council and central government for elections administered on their behalf face significant delays. The council currently holds \u00a3722,409.77 receivable for the last three elections, typical payment cycles extend to two years, creating a material cash flow timing issue.","category":"income","matters":"Extended payment delays on material government-owed debts materially affect council liquidity and cash flow planning","figures":"\u00a3722,409.77 owed for last 3 elections; up to 2 years payment delay","quote":"payments for these from Kent County Council and Central Government are not forthcoming and can take up to 2 years to be received. Currently Dover District Council is owed \u00a3722,409.77 for the last 3 elections carried out across the district.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance Committee","paper_title":"DDC - Quarterly Internal Audit Update Report 24-09-2026","paper_url":"https://dover.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s68358/68358.pdf","headline":"Housing rechargeable works orders totalling \u00a3194,731 have recovered only \u00a39,189, with critical audit recommendations unimplemented for 18 months","detail":"The council raised \u00a3194,731.21 in rechargeable works orders over four years but recovered only \u00a39,189.25 (4.7%). Recovery has effectively stopped in 2025-26 and 2026-27: \u00a38,328 raised in 2025-26 generated zero recovery; \u00a31,333 raised by June 2026 generated \u00a3220. A critical priority recommendation from March 2025 to pursue recovery of outstanding orders remains unimplemented; management's September 2026 comment notes the \"recovery/write-off process remains outstanding\" with revised completion pushed to March 2027.","category":"income","matters":"Poor cash collection on services provided indicates weak receivables control and cash flow risk","figures":"\u00a3194,731.21 raised; \u00a39,189.25 recovered; 2025-26: \u00a38,327.81 raised, \u00a30 recovered; 2026-27 (to June): \u00a31,333.20 raised, \u00a3220 recovered","quote":"Below is a table of the Rechargeable Works Orders that have been raised and the amounts received and this shows that for 25/26 onwards the value of Rechargeable Works Orders raised and also the recovery of monies is not taking place.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Dover","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":true,"headline":"PartnershipOne Business Rates Reliefs and Credits audit improved from Reasonable to Substantial assurance with only one medium-priority recommendation outstanding","lead":"Improved controls over business rates administration reduce revenue leakage risk and strengthen financial integrity","also":["18 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-10-05","n_findings":19,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"DDC - Quarterly Internal Audit Update Report 24-09-2026","top_paper_url":"https://dover.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s68358/68358.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":111,"news_drafts_total":1}}